  "summary": "Frosted panes floating over color: UI as architectural glazing.",
  "description": "Glassmorphism stacks interface cards as translucent frosted glass: background blur, 1px white rims, subtle inner highlights and layered depth — descends from Windows Vista Aero and iOS control centers, mainstreamed by macOS Big Sur (2020), Microsoft Fluent and the fintech boom (Revolut-era dashboards, every crypto app). The style sells premium software calm: content floats, color bleeds through panes, hierarchy reads as physical layering. Accessibility critiques (contrast collapse) keep it honest; variants persist in \"liquid glass\" OS updates and every dashboard template pack. Its material fantasy: software as architecture you can see through.",
  "culturalContext": "Glassmorphism mainstreamed depth-after-flat-design: Big Sur and Fluent made frosted panes the premium-software default, fintech dashboards adopted the look wholesale, and accessibility critiques keep the style in productive tension — software as architecture you can see through.",
